WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 5: Governance: where people decide — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.045
Software engineering 201. Lesson five.

2
00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:07.030
Governance: where people decide.

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.543
Picture this. Overnight, your agents wrote forty changes to a billing system.

4
00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:16.633
All pass their tests. One changes what customers are charged.

5
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.321
Who catches it? The tests? A person, reading all forty?

6
00:00:20.321 --> 00:00:22.735
Or a person, at a stop built for it?

7
00:00:22.735 --> 00:00:27.227
A stop built for it. Tests can't say whether you wanted the change.

8
00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.440
And nobody reads forty carefully.

9
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.567
Every era had a place where a person said yes.

10
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:38.160
Thirty years ago, typically, a change board, signing off a whole release, a few times a year.

11
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.210
Twenty years ago, more and more, a teammate, reviewing the code before it went in.

12
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:50.380
Ten years ago, an approval step in an automated pipeline: a pull request a reviewer approves, before it merges.

13
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.474
Each was sized for people writing the code.

14
00:00:53.474 --> 00:00:57.415
Now agents write changes faster than anyone can read them all.

15
00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:59.831
So that place can't just be inherited.

16
00:00:59.831 --> 00:01:01.230
It has to be designed.

17
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.524
So where does a person decide?

18
00:01:03.524 --> 00:01:06.052
A good rule of thumb is four questions.

19
00:01:06.052 --> 00:01:09.163
Does it move money? Does it touch customer data?

20
00:01:09.163 --> 00:01:13.376
Is it hard to undo? Is it a judgment call that no check can make?

21
00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:15.321
One yes, and a person decides.

22
00:01:15.321 --> 00:01:19.080
Four no's, and that kind of change can ship on its checks.

23
00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:24.071
Take our made-up example: the subscription-billing system from lesson one.

24
00:01:24.071 --> 00:01:26.455
Overnight, an agent drafts six things.

25
00:01:26.455 --> 00:01:28.901
Fix a typo on an internal admin screen.

26
00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:30.908
Add a missing test for renewals.

27
00:01:30.908 --> 00:01:35.110
Four no's, both times. They ship on their checks, and nobody waits.

28
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.917
Change how a late fee is calculated.

29
00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:40.920
That moves money: the finance owner decides.

30
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.243
Migrate the ledger. Money again, and hard to undo: the ledger's owner decides.

31
00:01:46.243 --> 00:01:48.837
Send customer emails to a new partner.

32
00:01:48.837 --> 00:01:51.430
Customer data: the data owner decides.

33
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:58.256
And it isn't only code. A refund over the limit: the agent prepares it, a support lead approves it.

34
00:01:58.256 --> 00:02:02.180
Under the limit, a person already decided, when they set it.

35
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.208
Six pieces of work. Four stops.

36
00:02:04.208 --> 00:02:05.320
Two never waited.

37
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.779
A stop only works if a person can clear it quickly.

38
00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:12.436
So the agent doesn't hand over a wall of diff and ask, okay?

39
00:02:12.436 --> 00:02:14.813
It prepares the decision. What changes.

40
00:02:14.813 --> 00:02:20.238
Why. The evidence, from the pipeline: last month's invoices, recomputed, old against new.

41
00:02:20.238 --> 00:02:24.627
The risk. And the way back. Now the finance owner decides about the fee.

42
00:02:24.627 --> 00:02:28.040
And an engineer still reviews the code that moves money.

43
00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.302
Then write it down: who approves what, in a file in the repository.

44
00:02:32.302 --> 00:02:34.346
Fees and prices: the finance owner.

45
00:02:34.346 --> 00:02:39.427
The ledger: its owner. The checks, the pipeline, and this file itself: always a person.

46
00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:42.113
Anything the list doesn't cover: stop and ask.

47
00:02:42.113 --> 00:02:45.325
And the pipeline enforces it, by what a change touches.

48
00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:49.120
The agent that wrote a change doesn't classify it, or approve it.

49
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.500
An old idea from accounting: whoever prepares a payment doesn't approve it.

50
00:02:53.850 --> 00:03:00.070
Stops cost something. A person on everything: people become the bottleneck, and yes turns into a rubber stamp.

51
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.190
A person on nothing: fast, until money moves unapproved.

52
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:08.441
So start strict, like lesson two: a person on every change.

53
00:03:08.441 --> 00:03:12.267
Then release one kind at a time, as its checks earn trust.

54
00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:15.960
And where an audit requires a review, that review stays.

55
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.074
So, to recap. Design where people decide.

56
00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:21.172
Make every stop quick to clear.

57
00:03:21.172 --> 00:03:25.570
And let the rest ship on its checks, one released kind at a time.

58
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.753
Which gives us rule five, for your agents.

59
00:03:28.753 --> 00:03:31.722
Before a change ships, find out who decides.

60
00:03:31.722 --> 00:03:42.381
Money, customer data, hard to undo, or a judgment call: stop for the person named in the repo, and bring the change, the evidence, the risk, and the way back.

61
00:03:42.381 --> 00:03:44.405
Not on the list: stop and ask.

62
00:03:44.405 --> 00:03:47.980
Ship on checks alone only what a person has released.

63
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:54.352
Now, your turn. Give your AI the last ten changes to a system you run, and the four questions.

64
00:03:54.352 --> 00:03:56.847
Ask it: which of these needed a person?

65
00:03:56.847 --> 00:03:59.469
Which could have shipped on checks alone?

66
00:03:59.469 --> 00:04:01.260
Then see where you disagree.

67
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.128
Ships on its checks. That phrase is carrying a lot.

68
00:04:05.128 --> 00:04:07.619
So how good are your checks, really?

69
00:04:07.619 --> 00:04:11.078
That's next: quality, feedback, and observability.

70
00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:12.600
See you in lesson six.
